# Merge and checkout rules for a SHARED .claude/, copied to .gitattributes by /setup when
# project.conf says PEOPLE="shared". Paths are relative to this repository's root, which is the
# .claude/ clone itself: git reads .gitattributes only from the repository CONTAINING the file.
#
# Union merge, for the APPEND-ONLY docs and ONLY those. Several people write to the tail of one
# file from separate machines, the most conflict-prone shape in git; union keeps both sides' lines
# with no conflict markers. The cost is that it NEVER conflicts: byte-identical lines are
# deduplicated, two entries interleave into one block that reads as coherent and is not, and two
# people editing the SAME line get BOTH versions. Hence the unique heading and unique timed closing
# stamp every entry needs: see CLAUDE.md, "Formatting for union merge", and run its audit.
#
# NOT the churn lists. In issues.md, hotfixes.md, traps.md and pipeline_backlog.md deleting an
# entry is normal, and union cannot express a deletion: a delete racing any edit to the same region
# is silently discarded and the entry comes back. Those take git's ordinary 3-way merge instead.
work/decisions.md merge=union
work/collab.md merge=union
work/collab_settled.md merge=union

# Shell scripts check out with LF whatever core.autocrlf says. A Windows clone with autocrlf=true
# rewrites every .sh to CRLF, the shebang then names an interpreter called `bash\r`, and the script
# dies pointing at a file you can see is right there. Without this line the fix is not durable: the
# next checkout, merge or stash puts the CRs straight back.
*.sh text eol=lf
